<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Avaris</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tell el-Dab'a</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Avaris was an ancient city in the north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a>, at the modern site of Tell el-Dab'a. From the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a> a large population of Levantine origin settled there, and it became the seat of the Fourteenth Dynasty and then the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> kings of the Fifteenth Dynasty, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmose-i">Ahmose I</a> captured it around 1550 BCE. Excavations since 1966 have found Minoan-style wall paintings there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/avaris.jpg" alt="Avaris" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ancient_Egypt_map-en.svg: Jeff Dahl derivative work: MinisterForBadTimes (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lower_Egypt-en.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
